# Test core library code

This project is a **reusable circuit-lab library** other sites may load from a CDN. Broken core behavior affects every consumer — treat important logic as must-test.

## What must be tested

Add or update Vitest tests in `tests/**/*.test.js` when changing:

- **Public / exported APIs** — anything `export`ed from `js/` that consumers or the app depend on
- **Pure logic** — config parsing/normalization (`lab-config.js`), continuity/simulation (`circuit.js`), grading (`grade.js`)
- **Validation & error paths** — invalid input, unknown refs, empty sources, contract breaks
- **Behavioral contracts** — “Front only energizes Front”, shared Rear/Side path, required components present

## What can skip heavy tests

Do **not** block on full canvas/UI coverage for:

- Konva drawing / layout polish (`components.js` visuals, wire rendering)
- Web Audio / sound playback (`sounds.js`)
- One-off app shell wiring that is not part of the library contract

If a change mixes UI and logic, still test the extracted pure logic.

## Workflow

1. Prefer pure, exportable helpers over logic buried in event handlers — easier to unit-test.
2. Mirror existing style: `tests/<area>.test.js`, import from `../js/...`, use `describe` / `it` / `expect`.
3. After editing core modules, run `npm test` and fix failures before finishing.
4. New exported behavior without a test is incomplete — add a focused test in the same change.

## Examples

```js
// ❌ BAD — change normalizeLabConfig / createGrader / simulator with no test update
export function normalizeLabConfig(raw) { /* new validation */ }

// ✅ GOOD — same change includes a Vitest case for the new contract
it("rejects demo wires that reference unknown components", () => {
  expect(() => normalizeLabConfig(raw)).toThrow(/unknown component/i);
});
```

```js
// ❌ BAD — important grading rule only lives inside a click handler
button.on("click", () => { /* complex pass/fail logic */ });

// ✅ GOOD — extract and test the rule; UI calls it
export function checkFrontPath(state) { /* ... */ }
```
